# @CHECKER@

A proof checker for the **@CALCULUS@** calculus.

Generated by Eudaimonia, a template for verified SMT proof checkers. This
directory is a complete Lake project and does not depend on the generator: it
can be moved anywhere, or made a repository of its own.

## Layout

Everything below is *this* project. It is standalone: it owns the compiler that
regenerates it and refers to nothing outside itself.

### The calculus

`@CALCULUS@.lean` is the library root; the modules are in `@CALCULUS@/`.

| module | | what it is |
| ------ | - | ---------- |
| `SmtEval.lean` | G | the primitives the embedding is evaluated over |
| `@CHECKER@Term.lean` | G | the term datatype of the calculus |
| `SmtModelDefs.lean` | G | what the model semantics is built from |
| `SmtValueOrder.lean` | G | the order on values |
| `SmtModel.lean` | G | the model semantics of SMT-LIB |
| `Spec.lean` | G | @CALCULUS@ ↔ SMT-LIB, and satisfiability |
| `@CHECKER@.lean` | G | the core checker |
| `Parser.lean` | G | the operator table for this signature |
| `Proofs/RuleLemmas.lean` | G | the dispatcher over the rules |
| `Api.lean` | F | what the executable does with a file — parse, then the three checks |
| `ApiChecks.lean` | F | each check is the theorem component it stands for. Proven |
| `ApiCorrect.lean` | F | soundness, about the text of a file, derived from `Proofs/Checker.lean` |
| `Diagnostics.lean` | F | where a rejected proof broke |
| `Proofs/Assumptions.lean` | F | the side conditions, and deciding them. Strengthen per rule as proofs need it |
| `Proofs/RuleSupport/Support.lean` | **H** | what every rule statement is written against. **Start here** |
| `Proofs/CheckerCore.lean` | **H** | correctness of the core, independent of the rules |
| `Proofs/Checker.lean` | **H** | the soundness theorem. Everything else exists to state it |
| `Proofs/Rules/` | G+H | one file per rule: statement generated, **proof yours** |

Three kinds of file, and knowing which is which is how you find the work.

| | kind | who wrote it | on `install-@CALCLOWER@.sh` |
| --- | ---- | ------------ | --------------------------- |
| **G** | **generated** | the compiler, from your signature | **overwritten** |
| **F** | **fixed** | copied in complete by the generator | left alone |
| **H** | **hand-written** | **you** — these arrive as stubs and `sorry`s | left alone |

**F is the category worth noticing.** Those files are copied in working and are
not yours to write: they are hand-written, but by the template rather than by
you. You *can* edit them, and there is no reason to unless you want to change
what the checker does. They are not regenerated from the signature either, so an
edit survives.

So the work is **H**, and there is less of it than the file count suggests.

`Proofs/Rules/` is the one file set that is both: a rule's statement is
generated with `sorry` for its proof, the proof goes in that same file, and a
reinstall **preserves** it.

`@CHECKER@.lean` and `@CHECKER@Term.lean` are named after the *checker* rather
than the calculus, because that is what they are — the core checking machinery
and the term datatype it runs on.

### Everything else

Everything here is **F** — copied in complete, working, and not yours to write.

| path | what it is |
| ---- | ---------- |
| `@FORMAT@.lean`, `@FORMAT@/` | reading the input proof format: an s-expression reader and a table-driven parser, independent of the calculus. `@CALCULUS@/Parser.lean` is the operator table that plugs into it |
| `@MINI@.lean`, `@MINI@/` | the reduced package, if this project has one: the same signature with a handful of rules and no parser, so proofs about the checker build in seconds |
| `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo` | the Eunoia signature |
| `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eos` | what its symbols mean |
| `install/defs/smt.eos` | the SMT-LIB semantics that is written against |
| `install/defs/profile.conf` | high-level facts about the calculus |
| `install/get-eo-compiler.sh` | fetch and build ethos-eoc, and ethos itself |
| `install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh` | compile the signature into `@CALCULUS@/` |
| `install/README.md` | what regeneration overwrites, and what it keeps |
| `scripts/` | `build.sh`, `build-rules.sh`, `rule-status.sh`, `check-proof-hygiene.sh`, `check-with-ethos.sh`, `run-ci.sh` |
| `docs/` | `calculus.md`, `development.md` |
| `test/regress/` | proofs, expected verdicts, and a runner |
| `Main.lean` | the executable: read a proof, report a verdict |
| `lakefile.toml`, `lean-toolchain` | the package and its pinned Lean version |

## Getting started

```bash
install/get-eo-compiler.sh       # once: build the Eunoia compiler
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh   # signature -> Lean
scripts/build.sh                 # check the result
```

Then [`docs/development.md`](docs/development.md) for the working loop and
[`install/README.md`](install/README.md) for what regeneration does and does not
overwrite.

## The contract @CALCULUS@ must meet

The correctness development is stated in terms of one operator of the signature,
named as a constructor of the `UserOp` enum that `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo`
compiles to.

| requirement | why |
| ----------- | --- |
| an operator **`and`** | the soundness statement is about the conjunction of a proof's assumptions |
| declared **`:right-assoc-nil true`** | Not decoration. The checker reads the input problem as an `and`-chain terminated by `true`, and every `:list`-premise rule depends on the nil the attribute generates. |
| **`and` sent to `SmtTerm.and`** by the semantics | A signature that declared `and` and translated it elsewhere would break soundness *silently*: nothing downstream re-checks that seam. |
| the Bool literals **`true`** / **`false`** | `false` is the refutation target — the checker's test is literally "has `false` been proven" — and `true` is the unit of the assumption chain. |

`install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh` checks all four before installing anything, so
a signature that does not meet the contract is refused with the reason rather
than failing later inside a generated file.

## Building

```bash
scripts/build.sh
```

That builds the library and the executable. Either alone:

```bash
scripts/build.sh @CALCULUS@     # the library
scripts/build.sh @EXE@          # the executable
```

`@CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect` is deliberately outside the default target: it depends
on every per-rule proof, which is far too expensive to build every time. Ask
for it by name, and expect it to take a long time:

```bash
scripts/build.sh @CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect
```

The checks CI runs:

```bash
scripts/run-ci.sh
```

## Running

```bash
lake exe @EXE@ <proof>
```

After a build the executable can be run directly, without Lake:

```bash
./.lake/build/bin/@EXE@ <proof>
```

## Status

After `install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh`, this is a **working checker**: it parses
the Eunoia proof format, runs the calculus, and reports one of three verdicts.
`test/regress/run.sh` exercises all of them and `scripts/run-ci.sh` passes.

Two axes, reported separately on purpose:

**What the checks find** — the verdict. `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Assumptions.lean` is
real, not a stub: a term is translatable when the semantics gives it a type. So
`correct`, `incorrect` and `incomplete` all mean what they say, and
`incomplete` is reachable for the right reason — a proof mentioning something
outside the formalization.

**How far the proof has got** — everything else.
`@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Checker.lean` holds `correct___eo_is_refutation` and it is a
`sorry`; each rule under `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Rules/` is a `sorry`; and
`@CALCULUS@/Proofs/RuleSupport/Support.lean` is the stub they are all stated
against.

So a verdict of `correct` today says *the checks passed*, not *this has been
proven*. `@CALCULUS@/ApiCorrect.lean` is already wired to the theorem, so
discharging it is what closes the gap — no other file changes. Watch
`scripts/rule-status.sh` and `scripts/run-ci.sh hygiene` for that, not the
verdict.
